lesson "The Fundamental Theorem of Calculus" {
  course: "calculus"
  unit: "Integration"
  difficulty: "Intermediate"
  icon: "MapPin"
  summary: "The result that ties the whole course together: exact area under a curve from nothing more than an antiderivative."
  skills: ["int-ftc"]

  slide "Derivatives and Integrals Are Opposites" {
    id: "i3-intro"
    cat: "The Theorem"
    > The **Fundamental Theorem of Calculus** is the payoff for everything so
    > far: it says the exact area under a curve doesn't need an infinite sum
    > of rectangles at all, just any antiderivative.
    >
    > $$\int_{a}^{b} f(x)\,dx = F(b) - F(a), \quad \text{where } F'(x) = f(x)$$
  }

  slide "The Shaded Region" {
    id: "i3-scene"
    cat: "The Theorem"
    > The area shaded below is $\int_{0}^{4} x^{2}\,dx$. Using $F(x) =
    > \dfrac{x^{3}}{3}$ as an antiderivative, the theorem says that area is
    > exactly $F(4) - F(0)$, no rectangles required.

    scene plane {
      x: [-0.5, 4.5]
      y: [-1, 10]
      grid
      axes
      curve f = x^2 { color: primary, width: 3 }
      area under = x^2 { from: 0, to: 4, color: accent, opacity: 0.2 }
      label at (1.5, 9) = "area = F(4) - F(0)" { color: accent }
    }
  }

  slide "Evaluate It Exactly" {
    id: "i3-numeric-exact"
    cat: "The Theorem"
    numeric {
      ask "Using $F(x) = \dfrac{x^{3}}{3}$, evaluate $\int_{0}^{4} x^{2}\,dx$ exactly."
      skill: "int-ftc"
      answer: 21.333
      tolerance: 0.01
      hint "F(4) - F(0) = 64/3 - 0."
      ! "F(4) - F(0) = 64/3 - 0 = 64/3 ≈ 21.33, matching the Riemann sum estimate from Lesson II, but exact this time."
    }
  }

  slide "A Simple Definite Integral" {
    id: "i3-numeric-simple"
    cat: "Applying FTC"
    numeric {
      ask "Evaluate $\int_{1}^{3} 2x\,dx$."
      skill: "int-ftc"
      answer: 8
      hint "An antiderivative of 2x is x². Evaluate at 3 and at 1."
      ! "[x²] from 1 to 3 is 9 - 1 = 8."
    }
  }

  slide "A Trig Definite Integral" {
    id: "i3-numeric-trig"
    cat: "Applying FTC"
    numeric {
      ask "Evaluate $\int_{0}^{\pi} \sin(x)\,dx$."
      skill: "int-ftc"
      answer: 2
      hint "An antiderivative of sin(x) is -cos(x)."
      ! "[-cos(x)] from 0 to π is -cos(π) - (-cos(0)) = 1 + 1 = 2."
    }
  }

  slide "The Other Half of the Theorem" {
    id: "i3-quiz-other-half"
    cat: "The Theorem"
    quiz {
      ask "The Fundamental Theorem also says $\dfrac{d}{dx}\left[\int_{a}^{x} f(t)\,dt\right] = ?$"
      skill: "int-ftc"
      - "$f(a)$" { why: "The result depends on x, the variable upper bound, not on the fixed lower bound a." }
      * "$f(x)$"
      - "$0$" { why: "Differentiating an accumulation function doesn't erase it, it just returns the original rate." }
      - "$F(x) - F(a)$" { why: "That's the value of the integral itself, not its derivative." }
      hint "Integrating and then differentiating should undo each other, just like differentiating then integrating."
      ! "Differentiating an accumulation function just returns the original function back: d/dx[∫ₐˣ f(t)dt] = f(x)."
    }
  }

  slide "Pick the Right Evaluation" {
    id: "i3-quiz-notation"
    cat: "The Theorem"
    quiz {
      ask "Which expression correctly evaluates $\int_{a}^{b} f(x)\,dx$ using an antiderivative F?"
      skill: "int-ftc"
      - "$F(a) - F(b)$" { why: "The bounds are swapped: it should be the upper bound minus the lower bound." }
      * "$F(b) - F(a)$"
      - "$F(a) + F(b)$" { why: "The two values are subtracted, not added." }
      - "$F(b) \cdot F(a)$" { why: "The Fundamental Theorem is a subtraction, not a product." }
      hint "Upper bound value minus lower bound value."
      ! "∫ₐᵇ f(x)dx = F(b) - F(a): plug in the upper bound, plug in the lower bound, subtract."
    }
  }

  slide "Put It Together" {
    id: "i3-capstone"
    cat: "Applying FTC"
    numeric {
      ask "Evaluate $\int_{0}^{2} \left(3x^{2} - 1\right) dx$."
      skill: "int-ftc"
      answer: 6
      hint "An antiderivative of 3x² - 1 is x³ - x."
      ! "[x³ - x] from 0 to 2 is (8 - 2) - (0 - 0) = 6."
    }
  }
}
